1
00:00:00,300 --> 00:00:02,290
$54,000 a year...

2
00:00:02,960 --> 00:00:03,670
for one bedroom.

3
00:00:05,150 --> 00:00:09,130
That’s roughly the average annual rent for a city-centre one-bedroom in New York.

4
00:00:09,910 --> 00:00:10,600
Before bills.

5
00:00:11,050 --> 00:00:11,710
Before food.

6
00:00:12,110 --> 00:00:13,660
Before you’ve even stepped outside.

7
00:00:15,400 --> 00:00:16,460
Think Dubai is expensive?

8
00:00:17,150 --> 00:00:18,540
Nine cities rank above it.

9
00:00:19,570 --> 00:00:24,220
And the cheapest city-centre rent on this list belongs to a quiet city that still outranks Dubai.

10
00:00:25,650 --> 00:00:26,770
So what are you actually paying for?

11
00:00:28,300 --> 00:00:33,560
Our ranking follows ECA International’s 2026 cost-of-living list for international employees.

12
00:00:34,490 --> 00:00:37,110
Rent figures are separate Numbeo estimates for a one-bedroom,

13
00:00:37,540 --> 00:00:38,290
in US dollars,

14
00:00:38,760 --> 00:00:39,370
before bills.

15
00:00:40,350 --> 00:00:40,640
10.

16
00:00:41,460 --> 00:00:41,790
Dubai.

17
00:00:44,040 --> 00:00:45,720
The Burj Khalifa sells you the dream.

18
00:00:46,320 --> 00:00:47,830
The rent tells you what it costs.

19
00:00:49,890 --> 00:00:53,170
Our central one-bedroom estimate is about $2,600 a month,

20
00:00:53,810 --> 00:00:56,890
though actual prices vary with the neighbourhood, the building and the view.

21
00:00:58,680 --> 00:01:00,970
Outside the centre, the estimate falls

22
00:01:01,370 --> 00:01:02,540
to roughly $1,500.

23
00:01:03,340 --> 00:01:06,470
That’s a gap of around $1,100 every month.

24
00:01:08,140 --> 00:01:10,010
So when you see a glossy apartment tour,

25
00:01:10,640 --> 00:01:12,710
ask which neighbourhood you’re actually being shown.

26
00:01:13,700 --> 00:01:15,600
The skyline can look identical in the thumbnail...

27
00:01:16,260 --> 00:01:17,490
while the commute and the bill

28
00:01:17,990 --> 00:01:18,670
look very different.

29
00:01:20,350 --> 00:01:20,690
9.

30
00:01:21,640 --> 00:01:22,320
San Francisco.

31
00:01:24,540 --> 00:01:28,680
A central one-bedroom costs around $3,700 a month in our snapshot.

32
00:01:29,440 --> 00:01:32,400
That’s $44,400 a year, before bills.

33
00:01:33,930 --> 00:01:35,740
The Golden Gate Bridge is free to admire.

34
00:01:36,560 --> 00:01:37,650
Keeping a roof over your head...

35
00:01:38,180 --> 00:01:39,000
is the expensive part.

36
00:01:40,730 --> 00:01:44,070
Outside the centre, the estimate drops to $2,700 a month.

37
00:01:45,040 --> 00:01:47,940
Over a year, that difference adds up to $12,000.

38
00:01:49,170 --> 00:01:50,890
So before falling for the skyline,

39
00:01:51,370 --> 00:01:52,880
compare the bedroom, the kitchen

40
00:01:53,390 --> 00:01:54,320
and the journey to work.

41
00:01:55,080 --> 00:01:56,650
How much of that rent buys convenience...

42
00:01:57,160 --> 00:01:58,670
and how much buys actual space?

43
00:02:00,350 --> 00:02:00,610
8.

44
00:02:01,840 --> 00:02:02,280
Singapore.

45
00:02:03,340 --> 00:02:10,010
Our central one-bedroom estimate is $3,200 a month. Outside the centre, it’s about $2,200.

46
00:02:11,090 --> 00:02:14,810
That’s a $1,000 gap before you’ve even decided how to get around.

47
00:02:15,930 --> 00:02:17,850
And that decision matters here.

48
00:02:18,850 --> 00:02:26,630
To own a new car, you’ll first need a Certificate of Entitlement, normally valid for 10 years and paid for separately from the vehicle itself.

49
00:02:27,890 --> 00:02:29,860
So compare life around the MRT

50
00:02:30,450 --> 00:02:31,900
with life built around a car.

51
00:02:32,800 --> 00:02:34,500
The skyline is the postcard.

52
00:02:35,370 --> 00:02:36,850
Your housing and transport choices

53
00:02:37,360 --> 00:02:38,670
decide the everyday bill.

54
00:02:40,350 --> 00:02:40,750
7.

55
00:02:41,800 --> 00:02:42,170
Bern.

56
00:02:43,580 --> 00:02:44,450
Here’s the surprise.

57
00:02:45,280 --> 00:02:47,860
This quiet Swiss city ranks above Dubai,

58
00:02:48,550 --> 00:02:51,170
yet it has the lowest central rent on our list:

59
00:02:51,660 --> 00:02:53,700
around $2,100 a month.

60
00:02:54,770 --> 00:02:55,640
Outside the centre,

61
00:02:56,110 --> 00:02:57,860
it’s about $1,600.

62
00:02:59,290 --> 00:03:00,190
Now add lunch.

63
00:03:01,400 --> 00:03:05,770
Numbeo puts a meal at an inexpensive restaurant at 24 Swiss francs...

64
00:03:06,220 --> 00:03:08,050
roughly $29 US dollars.

65
00:03:09,240 --> 00:03:12,980
Eat out regularly, and those ordinary purchases add up fast.

66
00:03:13,970 --> 00:03:17,730
That’s why the overall ranking and the rent comparison tell different stories.

67
00:03:18,840 --> 00:03:22,060
The old-town streets look a world away from Dubai’s towers.

68
00:03:22,850 --> 00:03:26,570
But a lower housing bill can still sit inside an expensive everyday life.

69
00:03:28,350 --> 00:03:28,790
6.

70
00:03:29,550 --> 00:03:30,100
Tel Aviv.

71
00:03:31,530 --> 00:03:33,810
The beach is the easy part of the sales pitch.

72
00:03:34,780 --> 00:03:36,600
The housing bill takes more thought.

73
00:03:37,020 --> 00:03:40,880
Our central one-bedroom estimate is about $2,300 a month,

74
00:03:41,390 --> 00:03:43,880
dropping to $1,700 outside the centre.

75
00:03:45,070 --> 00:03:48,130
That frees up roughly $600 a month for everything else.

76
00:03:49,660 --> 00:03:54,720
A seafront apartment and one on an ordinary residential street can feel like different cities.

77
00:03:55,560 --> 00:04:00,350
So compare the usable space, the condition of the building and the actual route to work.

78
00:04:01,430 --> 00:04:03,460
A coastal address sounds wonderful.

79
00:04:04,220 --> 00:04:06,670
But it’s the everyday flat you actually live in.

80
00:04:08,350 --> 00:04:08,880
5.

81
00:04:09,820 --> 00:04:10,380
Hong Kong.

82
00:04:12,570 --> 00:04:20,410
Residential towers crowd the harbour view. Yet government figures put about 40% of the territory inside country parks and special areas.

83
00:04:21,140 --> 00:04:22,830
The skyline is only part of the map.

84
00:04:24,490 --> 00:04:28,410
Our central one-bedroom estimate is around $2,400 a month,

85
00:04:29,230 --> 00:04:31,710
compared with $1,800 outside the centre.

86
00:04:33,120 --> 00:04:37,200
When it comes to the flat itself, compare floor area, not just bedrooms.

87
00:04:37,960 --> 00:04:40,950
A compact one-bedroom and a spacious one share the same label.

88
00:04:42,540 --> 00:04:46,670
Storage, daylight and kitchen layout show you what the monthly figure actually buys.

89
00:04:48,350 --> 00:04:48,740
4.

90
00:04:50,450 --> 00:04:50,950
Zurich.

91
00:04:53,380 --> 00:04:57,020
Our central one-bedroom benchmark is around $2,900 a month.

92
00:04:57,860 --> 00:05:00,720
Outside the centre, it falls to about $2,100.

93
00:05:01,550 --> 00:05:03,460
That's roughly $800 between the two.

94
00:05:06,290 --> 00:05:10,160
The trams, the lakeside and the old town make a convincing case for living here.

95
00:05:11,140 --> 00:05:14,530
But the decision still comes down to the money you keep after the essentials.

96
00:05:15,740 --> 00:05:18,120
Rent, food, transport and household bills

97
00:05:18,840 --> 00:05:21,070
all have to fit inside the same monthly budget.

98
00:05:22,340 --> 00:05:24,320
And that beautiful flat looks very different

99
00:05:25,120 --> 00:05:26,670
when there's little left after paying for it.

100
00:05:28,350 --> 00:05:28,910
3.

101
00:05:31,050 --> 00:05:31,510
London.

102
00:05:33,910 --> 00:05:35,910
Tower Bridge is the easy introduction.

103
00:05:37,180 --> 00:05:39,200
For a tenant, the harder number is the rent:

104
00:05:40,460 --> 00:05:46,320
about $2,900 US dollars a month for a central one-bedroom, or roughly £2,150.

105
00:05:48,650 --> 00:05:51,970
Outside the centre, the dollar estimate falls to $2,300.

106
00:05:53,650 --> 00:05:56,080
That saving matters, but so does the route to work.

107
00:05:58,220 --> 00:06:01,700
Count the changes, the walking and the fares before choosing the cheaper flat.

108
00:06:03,270 --> 00:06:06,570
Put the rent and the commute together, and you get a far more useful price.

109
00:06:08,350 --> 00:06:08,650
2.

110
00:06:09,570 --> 00:06:10,160
Geneva.

111
00:06:11,580 --> 00:06:13,890
The Jet d'Eau gives the city its signature view.

112
00:06:14,740 --> 00:06:18,230
Our central one-bedroom figure is about $2,900 a month,

113
00:06:18,760 --> 00:06:20,220
level with Zurich once rounded.

114
00:06:21,000 --> 00:06:23,500
Outside the centre, it's around $2,200.

115
00:06:24,520 --> 00:06:26,580
Yet Geneva ranks two places higher.

116
00:06:27,680 --> 00:06:31,020
That's another reason to keep the overall ranking separate from the rent comparison.

117
00:06:32,060 --> 00:06:34,080
Matching rents don't mean matching living costs.

118
00:06:35,170 --> 00:06:40,060
Before choosing between cities, compare your full budget in the currency you'll actually be paid in.

119
00:06:40,920 --> 00:06:42,840
A neat dollar figure makes comparison easy.

120
00:06:44,270 --> 00:06:46,670
Your own circumstances decide whether it's affordable.

121
00:06:48,350 --> 00:06:48,700
1.

122
00:06:49,800 --> 00:06:50,430
New York.

123
00:06:52,740 --> 00:06:53,890
Remember that opening figure?

124
00:06:55,120 --> 00:06:59,900
A city-centre one-bedroom averages roughly $4,500 a month in our snapshot.

125
00:07:02,200 --> 00:07:03,300
Multiply that by 12

126
00:07:04,250 --> 00:07:06,560
and you're at $54,000 a year.

127
00:07:09,120 --> 00:07:13,410
Outside the centre, the estimate is around $3,000 a month, or $36,000 a year.

128
00:07:15,060 --> 00:07:18,110
That's a difference of roughly $18,000 a year.

129
00:07:19,060 --> 00:07:20,710
And that's before the rest of daily life.

130
00:07:22,570 --> 00:07:25,590
So the layout, the daylight and the actual commute all matter.

131
00:07:27,110 --> 00:07:28,360
The famous view is a bonus.

132
00:07:30,110 --> 00:07:31,340
The bill arrives every month.

133
00:07:33,230 --> 00:07:34,470
Would the address be worth it to you?

134
00:07:36,400 --> 00:07:40,260
Across these cities, the useful number is what you have left after the essentials.

135
00:07:41,950 --> 00:07:42,870
Compare like with like,

136
00:07:43,690 --> 00:07:44,530
include the commute,

137
00:07:45,360 --> 00:07:47,450
and treat any average as a starting point.

138
00:07:49,590 --> 00:07:51,330
Which city would you pay a premium for?

139
00:07:53,160 --> 00:07:57,270
Tell us in the comments, and subscribe for more comparisons of what your money actually buys.
